{"id": "f63b0e4c-3242-40ce-9d5a-8843ad08d505", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check this from the account side: your agent's runtime logs and error codes, the network path out to OpenAI, and any changes since this morning. That should show whether the failures point to the provider or to your side. I'll report back with a verdict.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_poCnZBSm0QxQ50AQ1YFxR4", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "openai-failure-triage", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["searching-logs", "exploring-metrics", "exploring-traces", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-deployments", "discovering-topology", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Triage whether a ~33% failure rate on outbound calls to the OpenAI API (direct, NOT Bedrock) is caused by the provider (OpenAI) or by the customer's own infrastructure/application. Produce an evidence-backed verdict: provider / customer infrastructure / application / undetermined, with confidence.",
  "context": "The customer's agent calls api.openai.com directly for inference. Since this morning about a third of calls fail. Their own application metrics look normal. They want triage from what is observable in the AWS accounts associated with this agent space (account 123456789012, region us-east-1 as the agent space home; discover other associated accounts/regions and where the agent runs: Lambda, ECS, EKS, EC2, etc.). You cannot check OpenAI's status page from here, so decide from account-side evidence. Do not use any Bedrock-specific assumptions. Do not perform mutating AWS operations.",
  "resources": ["Discover the agent workload's compute, log groups, and its egress path (NAT gateways, VPC endpoints, proxies, security groups, NACLs, route tables, DNS/Route 53 Resolver, Secrets Manager/SSM holding the OpenAI API key)"],
  "timeframe": "2026-11-24T20:00:00Z/2026-11-25T16:38:00Z (compare failures-start window this morning vs. prior baseline)",
  "criteria": "1) In application/runtime logs, extract the error breakdown for OpenAI calls: HTTP status codes (429 rate limit/quota, 401/403 auth or key, 5xx 500/502/503/529, 400), timeouts, connection resets, DNS or TLS errors; note onset time, whether failures are intermittent per-request vs per-instance/AZ/subnet/NAT, and any 'x-request-id' / retry-after / rate-limit headers or error messages in logs. A 429 insufficient_quota or 401 points at the customer's account/key; 5xx/overloaded across all instances and AZs points at the provider; timeouts/resets concentrated on one NAT/AZ/subnet point at customer network. 2) Egress path health: NAT gateway metrics (ErrorPortAllocation, PacketsDropCount, ConnectionAttemptCount vs EstablishedCount, IdleTimeoutCount, bytes), ENI/instance network limits, VPC Flow Logs rejects if available, Route 53 Resolver query logs, proxy/firewall (Network Firewall, security group, NACL) changes. 3) Changes since the prior baseline: deployments, config/env var changes, secret rotation, SDK/library version bumps, IAM/network changes, scaling events (use CloudTrail via the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill). 4) Resource saturation (CPU/memory/throttling/concurrency limits, connection pool exhaustion) even if the customer says their metrics look normal. 5) Check AWS Health events for networking/service issues in the relevant Regions. Report: verdict, the evidence for and against each of provider/infrastructure/application, what you could NOT check, and the 1-3 most useful next steps. Keep the final report concise."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI API failures: logs, egress network, recent changes"}], "created_at": null}

